<p style="color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
By <a href="/en/urban-gavelin/" rel="author">Urban Gavelin</a>, <time datetime="2026-08-17">August 17, 2026</time>, Pipeline &amp; Sales Leadership
</p>
<h1>What Happens in the Weekly Meeting Decides Everything Else</h1>
<img src={coverImage} alt="What Happens in the Weekly Meeting Decides Everything Else" width="1280" height="720" class="hero-img" />
<p class="lead"><strong>I've spent more time on weekly meetings than on almost anything else in my career. A large part of that time was wasted. This letter is about why, and about what separates a weekly meeting that drives deals from one that merely reports on them.</strong></p>

<div class="section">
<h2>The Meeting Isn't the Problem, It's What Gets Measured</h2>
<p>A typical weekly meeting in a sales team goes like this: everyone reviews the pipeline. Each rep reports where their deals stand. The manager asks follow up questions. Someone promises to reach out to the customer. Next week, the same thing happens again.</p><p>The problem is that the conversation is built on belief. How far along is it really? Will it close this month? What do you think? Belief is not a metric.</p><p>I ran meetings like this for years. We had numbers, sometimes good numbers, but I could never point to what actually caused them. We rolled out changes, ran campaigns, and pitched deals, and I often wondered if any of it mattered. It was nearly impossible to measure the outcome of a single activity. Working hard without being able to measure anything isn't discipline. It's living on hope.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>What Changed</h2>
<p>The difference didn't come from a new tool. It came from changing the questions we asked.</p><p>Instead of asking what a rep believed about a deal, we asked what had to be true for it to move forward. Has the decision maker been identified? Have we heard the objection in the customer's own words? Is there a date the customer themselves committed to?</p><p>Those questions can be answered with a yes or a no. And yes and no can be counted. After one quarter, two things happened. The forecast became accurate. And for the first time, we could see which activities actually moved something.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>Why Rhythm Matters More Than Content</h2>
<p>The decisive factor wasn't that the questions were clever. It was that they were the same every single week.</p><p>A criterion that gets tested every week becomes a habit within a quarter. The same criterion, tested only when someone happens to remember, becomes nothing at all.</p><p>This is also why sales systems rarely die from bad design. They die from a missing rhythm. Someone builds the process, everyone agrees it's good, and then a busy month hits and the review gets cancelled twice. By the third time, nobody needs to cancel it, because everyone has already stopped preparing.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>What You Do on Monday</h2>
<ul><li>Write down two criteria for each stage of your sales process. Two sentences are enough, they don't need to be perfect.</li><li>Swap out one question at your next weekly meeting. Remove 'how do you think it's going' and add 'which criterion is still missing'.</li><li>Book the review twelve weeks out right now, and treat it like a customer meeting. It doesn't get cancelled.</li></ul><p>It takes an hour to prepare and will feel stiff the first few times. That passes.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">What's the fastest way to start changing our weekly meeting?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">Pick one stage in your sales process and write down two clear, yes or no criteria for moving past it. Replace one belief based question in your next meeting with a question tied to one of those criteria and see how the conversation shifts.</p>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">How do we know if the new questions are actually working?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">Give it a full quarter and compare forecast accuracy before and after. If the criteria are specific and tested every week, you should start seeing fewer surprises and a clearer link between activities and outcomes.</p>
</div>
</div>

